<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements: permissions baked into the app's signature (docs/desktop.md §6). Two, each
  an exception to the hardened runtime (on, in electron-builder.yml), which otherwise
  protects the app from having its memory or its libraries tampered with.

  allow-jit: V8, Electron's JavaScript engine, compiles JavaScript to machine code while it
    runs, which needs memory that is written and then executed. The hardened runtime
    forbids that memory without this, and the app dies at launch with "Failed to reserve
    virtual memory for CodeRange" (found by testing, 21 Nov 2026). It's the narrow form;
    the broader allow-unsigned-executable-memory isn't needed.

  disable-library-validation: the hardened runtime lets a program load only code signed by
    the same team. Electron's own frameworks carry Electron's team signature, and an
    ad-hoc-signed app has no team at all, so without this they can't be loaded. A Developer
    ID-signed app wouldn't need it; that's the $99 a year.

  The alternative to both, switching the hardened runtime off, would drop every other
  protection it gives.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
